{
  "response": "# Verdict: ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component implements a robust search form with correct semantic structure and a well-designed live region strategy for status updates (loading, success, empty). However, the \"Search Results\" list is rendered as static text rather than interactive elements (links/buttons), which creates a functional barrier for keyboard users. Additionally, focus management is absent when results update.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1.  **ARIA Pattern**: The live region uses `role=\"status\"`. I predicted this might be insufficient for search results and that a specific `aria-label` on the results list was missing.\n2.  **Focus Management**: I predicted focus would remain on the submit button after results load, leaving screen reader users disoriented.\n3.  **Semantic HTML**: I predicted the list items (`<li>`) might lack interactive wrappers (links) if this is a search widget.\n4.  **Labeling**: I predicted potential redundancy or conflict between the visible label and `aria-label`.\n\n**Actual Findings vs Predictions**:\n*   *Prediction 1 Confirmed*: The results list lacks an accessible name (`aria-label`), making it ambiguous to screen readers.\n*   *Prediction 2 Confirmed*: Focus does not move to the results; it stays on the trigger.\n*   *Prediction 3 Confirmed*: List items are static text, not links. This is a critical functional gap for a search widget.\n*   *Prediction 4 Partially Confirmed*: The label and `aria-label` are redundant but safe (both announce).\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks access)\n\n1.  **Search Results List Items Are Not Interactive**\n    *   **Evidence**: `SearchResults.jsx:60-64`. The map function renders `<li>` elements containing only text (`<h3>`, `<p>`). No `<a>` or `<button>` is present.\n    *   **User Group**: Keyboard-only users, Screen reader users.\n    *   **Issue**: If this component represents search results, the items are not navigable. A keyboard user cannot tab into a result to visit it. A screen reader user will read the text but receive no indication that it is actionable.\n    *   **Fix**: Wrap the result content in an anchor tag (`<a href={result.url}>`) or button. If the entire item is clickable, wrap the `<li>` content in the link.\n    *   **WCAG/APG**: 2.1.1 Keyboard (All functionality available via keyboard).\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly degrades experience)\n\n2.  **Focus Does Not Move to Results After Update**\n    *   **Evidence**: `SearchResults.jsx:48`. The state updates (`setResults`) re-render the list, but no focus management code exists. Focus remains on the submit button or input field.\n    *   **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n    *   **Issue**: When results appear, the screen reader announces \"Found X results\" (via the live region), but the user's context is still at the search form. They must manually tab past the results to find anything else on the page.\n    *   **Fix**: Move focus to the results list container or the first result item when `results.length > 0`. Use a ref (`useRef`) to target the element and call `.focus()`.\n    *   **WCAG/APG**: 2.4.3 Focus Order (Focus moves logically).\n\n3.  **Results List Lacks Accessible Name**\n    *   **Evidence**: `SearchResults.jsx:58`. The `<ul className=\"results-list\">` has no `aria-label` or visible heading associated with it directly.\n    *   **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n    *   **Issue**: Screen readers will announce \"List\" without context. It is unclear if this list contains navigation, actions, or data.\n    *   **Fix**: Add `aria-label=\"Search results\"` to the `<ul>` element.\n    *   **WCAG/APG**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction but workaround exists)\n\n4.  **Redundant Labeling on Input**\n    *   **Evidence**: `SearchResults.jsx:30-31`. The input has both a visible `<label>` (visually hidden via CSS) and an `aria-label=\"Search query\"`.\n    *   **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n    *   **Issue**: The screen reader will announce \"Search, Search query edit\". This is redundant but not harmful. It's safer to keep both than to remove the label if the `htmlFor` association breaks later.\n    *   **Fix**: Keep as is (safe redundancy) or remove `aria-label` if the visible label is sufficient.\n\n5.  **List Styling Removes Visual Cues**\n    *   **Evidence**: `SearchResults.css:46`. `.results-list { list-style: none; }`.\n    *   **User Group**: Low vision users.\n    *   **Issue**: Removing bullet points removes a visual cue for list structure. This is acceptable if the design intent is clean cards, but ensure the border/padding provides enough separation to distinguish items visually.\n    *   **Fix**: Ensure borders/backgrounds clearly separate list items (already present in CSS).\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n*   **Interactive Elements**: The results are static text. A search result component implies navigation.\n*   **Focus Management Code**: No mechanism to move focus after the async state update.\n*   **ARIA Label on List**: The list is unnamed.\n*   **`role=\"search\"` on Form**: While not strictly required, adding `role=\"search\"` to the `<form>` explicitly identifies it as a search widget for assistive technology (optional but recommended).\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n*   **Screen reader user**: Hears \"Search form\". Submits. Hears \"Searching...\". Then \"Found 3 results\". Reads list items (\"Result 1\", etc.). *Critique*: The list is announced as just a list; context is missing. Items are not clickable.\n*   **Keyboard-only user**: Can tab to input, type, hit Enter. Focus stays on button. Tabbing forward goes past the results (if they aren't links) or into them (if they are). *Critique*: If items aren't links, the user cannot access the content of the search results at all.\n*   **Low vision user**: Contrast is good (`#0066cc` on white). Text size is adequate. *Critique*: None found.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\nThe component is structurally sound regarding the form and live region implementation (which is actually quite good). The \"No results\" state handling via the live region is excellent design.\n\nHowever, the **Critical Finding** regarding non-interactive list items is a functional failure for a search widget. If this code renders static text only, it passes. If it renders results intended for navigation, it fails keyboard accessibility entirely. I am issuing **ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS** because the form logic and state communication are correct, but the result presentation is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n1.  Is the intent of this component to display static text or provide navigable results?\n2.  Does the parent page handle focus management for the search region, or does this component need to manage it internally?",
